# `_quoted_atom_start` is one byte with a look at the next one, and it is the
# last thing standing between elixir and a whole parse.
#
# The scanner advances past `:`, calls `mark_end`, and then only *peeks*: it
# returns true when the next byte is `"` or `'` and false otherwise. So the
# token is the colon alone, the quote stays the grammar's own, and the whole
# external is a spelling plus trailing context - `outside.Provision`, not a
# hand. `: "a"` is a colon before a string and `:"a"` is an atom, and only the
# byte after the colon tells them apart, which is the entire reason it is C.
#
# The interpolation is here on purpose. It is the shape the corpus actually
# holds - `router.ex` has `:"__match_route_#{verb}__"` at byte 22,229 and
# `:"__pipe_through#{...}"` at 24,175, and those two were where the parse stopped
# once the newline seam was seated. A quoted atom with no `#{}` would pass on a
# close that swallowed the body.
#
# Claims are tree-sitter's tree over these 17 bytes.
roots 1
mends 0
holds quoted_atom
holds interpolation
lacks ERROR
spans quoted_atom 7 16
spans interpolation 10 14
